{define: A6+ base-fret 1 frets x x 2 2 2 3} 
{define: A5st base-fret 1 frets 0 0 7 6 5 5} 
{define: AA5st base-fret 1 frets x x 2 2 2 5}
{define: D4 base-fret 1 frets x x 0 2 3 3}
{define: Dsus2 base-fret 1 frets 0 0 0 2 3 0} 
{t: BIG WEEKEND}
{t: Tom Petty}
{artist: Tom Petty}
{composer: Tom Petty}


https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xBZr5nq0Yd0  (Capo 0)

TIP:  The (D) to (D4) to (D) bits are easy (actually there's also a (Dsus2) in there but we're gonna forget about that until the outro).  So just keep the (D) formation and add your pinky to the 1st string/3rd fret for the (D4) and two notes that follow and then lift to return to (D).

TIP:  Likewise, the (A) to (A6+) to (A6) to (A) series is also easy and fun to play.  Keep the (A) formation throughout.  Add your pinky (don't play a barre) to the 1st string/3rd fret to play the (A6+) and then just slide it back one fret for the (A6), then remove the pinky to get back to the (A) before jumping to the (D).  Easy cheesy.  (The (A6+) likely won't transpose.)

Now if you want to get fancy you can emulate Tom's slide trill ("st" in the lyrics) that comes after the (A) on "end" and "dust" by playing an (A) on the 5th fret (played like an (F) or, and I think this one is easier, the (AA5) chord in which you barre the 2nd fret and stretch your pinky over the cover the 1st string of the 5th fret and can then to complete the chorus tap the 1st string/3rd fret with your middle finger for the (A6+), lift for the (A6), then lift up slightly off the barre to open the 1st string for the (A), and finish with a regular (D).

TIP: As for the outro, you'll end the chorus in (D).  Lift off the ring finger go to from (D) to  (Dsus2) and replace it to get back to (D).  One strum each.

           
